editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Let the state measure the cost to transform a source prefix into a target prefix. Consider deleting the last source character, inserting the last target character, or aligning them with a match or replacement. Initialize the empty-prefix row with insertion costs and the empty-prefix column with deletion costs. Keep two rows and use 64-bit arithmetic.

## Walk through the sample
Changing cat to cut can replace a by u for cost 9. Deleting a and inserting u costs 3 + 2 = 5, so the DP must compare all three transitions even when a substitution looks natural.

## Complexity
Time O(|a||b|), space O(|b|).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string a, b;
    long long ins, del, rep;
    cin >> a >> b >> ins >> del >> rep;
    vector<long long> prev(b.size() + 1), cur(b.size() + 1);
    for (int j = 1; j <= int(b.size()); ++j)
        prev[j] = j * ins;
    for (int i = 1; i <= int(a.size()); ++i) {
        cur[0] = i * del;
        for (int j = 1; j <= int(b.size()); ++j)
            cur[j] = min({prev[j] + del, cur[j - 1] + ins,
                          prev[j - 1] + (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1] ? 0 : rep)});
        swap(prev, cur);
    }
    cout << prev.back() << '\n';
}
~~~
